color: rgba(255, 89, 98, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">2.5%</span> of psychology papers' Results sections, as of 2020-2024. </p>Among 2020-2024 papers containing <span  \"><i>\"members\" </i></span>, <span style=\"color: rgba(94, 184, 242, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">28.5%</span> of significant p-values (p < .05) are fragile (.01 \u2264 p < .05) \u2013 i.e., barely fall under the standard significance threshold. This is a higher than the typical psychology paper (median shown for reference). Relative to other words, <span  \"><i>\"members\" </i></span> papers are in the top <span style=\"color: rgba(3, 152, 252, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">84<sup>th</sup></span> percentile in terms of fragile p-value percentage (lower = better). Note, p-values highly predict replicability (see <a href=\"../assets/pdf/Bogdan_2025_PsyChange_Manuscript_SuppMat.pdf\">the associated paper</a>).","statement1":"As of 2020-2024, papers containing <span  \"><i>\"members\" </i></span> tend to be published in higher impact factor journals (<span style=\"color: rgba(105, 219, 99, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">54<sup>th</sup></span> percentile among all words)  but tend to be cited less (<span style=\"color: rgba(255, 168, 82, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">16<sup>th</sup></span> percentile word). In general, this word is positively associated with university ranking, such that higher-ranked institutions are more likely to publish papers containing <span  \"><i>\"members\" </i></span> (<span style=\"color: rgba(170, 117, 250, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">53<sup>rd</sup></span> percentile). "}